Asynchronous programming in C# is a way to write code that can execute long-running operations without blocking the main thread of execution. When you write asynchronous code, you can use the `async` and `await` keywords to indicate that a given method or block of code can be executed asynchronously.

The `async` keyword is used to define an asynchronous method, which is a method that can contain an `await` operator. When the `await` operator is encountered within an asynchronous method, the method's execution is suspended until the awaited task completes. During this time, the main thread of execution can continue processing other code.

When the awaited task completes, the remaining steps in the asynchronous method are added to the calling thread's task queue and executed when the thread becomes idle. This means that the calling thread can also continue executing other methods while the asynchronous method is waiting for its awaited task to complete.

Asynchronous code can improve performance and responsiveness in your application, because it allows a single thread to execute multiple methods concurrently.

Here is an example of how you could use the `async` and `await` keywords to write an asynchronous method in C#:
```
public async Task<string> GetStringFromServerAsync()
{
    // The HttpClient.GetStringAsync method is asynchronous, so we can use await with it.
    string result = await httpClient.GetStringAsync("https://example.com");
    return result;
}
```
Async methods return a `Task` or a `Task<T>` (where `T` is some type), which represents the result of the operation. This allows you to use `await` on the result, which schedules any additional work (like continuations) to be executed after the task completes executing.

