Sure, I can provide an example to demonstrate why moving a `shared_ptr` in C++ is generally faster than copying it.

In C++, the `shared_ptr` class is designed to manage the lifetime of a dynamically allocated object. It uses reference counting to keep track of how many pointers (smart pointers) are pointing to the same object. When a `shared_ptr` is moved (assigned), the new object takes ownership of the underlying resource, and the old object no longer owns it. This implies that moving a `shared_ptr` will increment the reference count of the underlying resource, while copying it will create a new instance with its own reference count and manage the original and the copy as separate resources.

The process of moving a `shared_ptr` is generally faster because it does not involve the creation of a new object with its own overhead (e.g., memory allocation). Instead, it transfers the ownership and increases the reference count of the existing underlying resource.

Here's a small example to illustrate the difference:
```cpp
#include <iostream>
#include <memory>
#include <cassert>

class MyClass {
public:
    MyClass(int value) : value(value) {}

    void print() const {
        std::cout << "MyClass: " << value << std::endl;
    }

private:
    int value;
};

int main() {
    // Create a shared_ptr with a new MyClass
    std::shared_ptr<MyClass> original_ptr(new MyClass(10));

    // Copy the shared_ptr
    std::shared_ptr<MyClass> copy_ptr = original_ptr;

    // Move the shared_ptr (note the = operator)
    std::shared_ptr<MyClass> move_ptr = std::move(original_ptr);

    // Release the original_ptr to demonstrate reference counting
    original_ptr.release();

    // Both copy_ptr and move_ptr still have a reference to the underlying resource

    // Test with unique_ptr to ensure we have only one reference left
    std::unique_ptr<MyClass> unique_ptr = std::move(move_ptr);

    // Both copy_ptr and move_ptr should now share the same underlying resource
    // Test with shared_ptr:: display_locked to ensure only one reference left
    assert(original_ptr.display_unlocked() == *copy_ptr);
    assert(original_ptr.display_unlocked() == *move_ptr);
    assert(original_ptr.display_unlocked() == *unique_ptr);

    // Free the shared_ptr resources
    original_ptr.release();
    copy_ptr.release();
    move_ptr.release();
    unique_ptr.reset();

    return 0;
}
```
In this example, moving a `shared_ptr` (line 20) is faster than copying it (line 11) because it does not involve the creation of a new object with its own overhead. The moving process merely transfers the ownership of the underlying resource and increases its reference count.

Please note that the performance improvement from moving a `shared_ptr` instead of copying it is not always significant. In certain cases, the difference might be negligible. However, it's good practice to use moves when possible to avoid unnecessary object creation.

