\section{Introduction}

Natural Language Generation (NLG) is a subfield of Artificial Intelligence (AI) that deals with the automatic production of human-like text from structured data or algorithms. Evaluating the quality of NLG systems has always been a challenging task due to the complexity and ambiguity of natural language. Human evaluation, which involves the assessment of generated texts by human raters, is considered the most robust and reliable method for evaluating NLG systems \citep{belz2006comparing}. This section presents an overview of human evaluation in NLG, discussing its importance, parameters, types, limitations, and challenges.

\section{What is human evaluation, and why do we need it?}

Human evaluation is a method of assessing the quality of NLG systems by having human raters judge the output of the system based on specific quality criteria. The main advantage of human evaluation is that it provides a subjective measure of the quality of the generated text, which is often more relevant than objective measures. NLG systems are designed to produce texts that are understandable and useful to humans, and human evaluation is the most appropriate way to assess whether these goals have been achieved \citep{belz2006comparing}.

Despite the intuitive appeal of human evaluation, automatic evaluation methods, such as correlation metrics like ROUGE \citep{lin2004rouge}, have been proposed as alternatives. However, automatic metrics often lack the sensitivity to capture specific aspects of NLG quality, such as fluency, coherence, and fidelity \citep{reiter-etal-2010-building}. Moreover, automatic metrics are often domain-specific, and their performance may vary depending on the type of text and evaluation task \citep{pagnoni-etal-2021-understanding}. Hence, human evaluation is still needed to provide a more comprehensive and reliable assessment of NLG systems.

\section{Parameters of human evaluation}

Human evaluation can be conducted in different ways, depending on the specific NLG task and the research question being addressed. The following subsections present some of the key parameters of human evaluation in NLG.

\subsection{Quality criteria}

The quality of an NLG system can be assessed using different criteria, such as fluency, coherence, readability, accuracy, and relevance \citep{belz2006comparing}. Fluency refers to the naturalness of the generated text, coherence to the logical relationship between the sentences, readability to the ease of understanding, accuracy to the correspondence between the generated text and the underlying data or algorithm, and relevance to the goal of the NLG task. Quality criteria can be evaluated using various evaluation frameworks, as discussed in the following subsection.

\subsection{Evaluation framework}

The evaluation framework determines the way in which quality criteria are assessed. Two main frameworks are direct assessment and comparative assessment \citep{belz-kow-2010-comparing}. Direct assessment involves evaluating a generated text alone, without referencing other candidates, and is typically used for evaluating readability and coherence. Comparative assessment, on the other hand, involves evaluating the relative quality of two or more texts, and is typically used for evaluating fluency, accuracy, and relevance. Evaluation frameworks can also include hybrid approaches that combine direct and comparative assessment.

\subsection{Design}

The design of a human evaluation experiment refers to the specific procedure used to collect judgments from human raters. Two main designs are absolute and comparative \citep{belz2020disentangling}. In absolute design, raters assess the quality of a single text or a set of texts without comparing them to other texts. In comparative design, raters compare pairs or sets of texts and make decisions based on their relative quality. The design choice depends on the evaluation framework and the research question being addressed.

